/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/ IPC server half. Requires the `ipc` feature (on by default).
/
/ `\port` is an ordinary statement, so a script can open one directly — the
/ process keeps serving requests after this script's last statement, exactly
/ as if `-i` had dropped it into the REPL. Run:
/
/   cargo run --load-demo -- examples/ipc_server.qpl
/
/ Then, in another terminal, run examples/ipc_client.qpl against it.
/ A bare `\port` (no argument) closes the listener again; typed at the REPL
/ prompt this also works the same way, mid-session.
/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/ `\port`'s argument is an ordinary value-context expression, not just a
/ literal — it can be a variable, arithmetic, or (as here) a conditional that
/ picks the port at runtime. String equality has no scalar `=` (that's `like`,
/ an exact match with no wildcards in the pattern — see the "Operator
/ reference" in README.md), which is why this compares with `like`, not `=`.
env: "test"
port: ?[env like "test"; 5001; 5002]
log "demo tables `trades` / `quotes` are ready — opening \\port " str$port
\port port
